From: SqueezeFS-Zig <sqz@localhost>
Date: Mon, 20 Oct 2026 04:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] sqz: fuse: io_uring: a cheaper request round trip (one header copy per direction, per-entry zero-copy scratch, one lock section for commit-end-and-fetch, no attr_version bump on a read's completion)

PROPOSED (scripts/kernel/proposed/, not in the series): compile-checked
(W=1) as an out-of-tree fuse module against the 7.2.8 omarchy-sqz tree
with 0001-0030 applied; NOT booted, NOT measured. The owner builds and
boots; the daemon-side probe plan is below.

Why (docs/evidence/rung-10-fuse-boundary.md). A FUSE-over-io_uring round
trip with no filesystem behind it (the daemon answers a 4 KiB O_DIRECT
READ at its delivery CQE with a header-only commit) costs 3.0 us of host
CPU per read on this kernel: fio 1.3-1.7 us, the daemon worker 1.9-2.3 us
of which ~0.3 us is userspace. The worker's kernel share, by self time
per read (perf, 2.35 M reads/s, 8 workers):

  spinlocks (_raw_spin_lock + queued slowpath)          0.34 us
  header copies (3 copy_to_iter per send, 2 copy_from_iter
    per commit, each an iov advance over the headers bvec,
    fuse_uring_args_to_ring self, memcpy)               ~0.25 us
  request end + completion to the app's ring
    (fuse_uring_req_end, fuse_put_request, io_complete_rw,
    io_req_local_work_add)                              ~0.30 us
  zero-copy register/unregister (kzalloc + kcalloc +
    2 kfree + memcg hooks per request, io_free_rsrc_node,
    fuse_zc_pages_release)                              ~0.18 us
  io_uring plumbing, the park/wake                      ~0.45 us

This patch takes the FUSE-uring-specific terms that are not the protocol:

1. One header copy per direction. The send stages fuse_in_header, the
   op header and fuse_uring_ent_in_out in a stack image of struct
   fuse_uring_req_header and writes the 288 bytes once; the commit reads
   the image once and takes fuse_out_header and ent_in_out from it.
   Three advance-and-copy passes per send and two per commit (16-40
   bytes each, one bvec iteration each) become one each. The per-type
   copy helpers and enum fuse_uring_header_type go.

2. Per-entry zero-copy scratch. fuse_uring_set_up_zero_copy() allocated a
   folio carrier and a bvec array per request and freed both at the
   unregister (the carrier through the imu's release callback). Each
   entry now owns one carrier and one bvec array for up to 32 folios
   (4 KiB-128 KiB requests; a larger request allocates as before and
   amortizes it). The carrier's lifetime is the imu's, so it is claimed
   with an acquire cmpxchg (FREE -> BUSY) at registration and handed back
   by the release (BUSY -> FREE, release ordering); an entry freed while
   its carrier is BUSY orphans it (xchg ORPHAN) and the release frees it.
   Exactly one of the two frees it, never both.

3. One queue->lock section for the plain commit's end-and-fetch.
   fuse_uring_req_end() detached the request and credited the queue under
   the lock, fuse_uring_get_next_fuse_req() took it again to make the
   entry available and assign the next request. fuse_uring_commit_end_fetch()
   does both in one section, then unregisters the zc slot, ends the
   request and prepares the next. The retain arm and the error arms keep
   the old sequence. The daemon's commit path takes the queue lock three
   times per request instead of four, against the submitting CPU's
   fuse_uring_queue_bq_req() on the same lock.

4. fuse_aio_complete() bumps fc->attr_version (a connection-wide atomic)
   under fi->lock for every completed async direct I/O; the synchronous
   path bumps only for writes (fuse_write_update_attr). A read changes
   nothing the bump guards, and at 1.25 M reads/s the atomic and the
   per-file lock are contended across every CPU completing one file's
   reads. Bump for writes only. (This is fs/fuse/file.c, not the uring
   transport; it is here because the profile put it on the same path.)

Expected saving, from the profile: 0.25-0.35 us of the worker's ~2.0 us
kernel share per read (the header copies ~0.1, the zc allocations ~0.1,
the lock trip and the attr_version bump ~0.05-0.15), i.e. 12-17 % of the
round trip's kernel cost and 4-5 % of today's 6.4 us daemon cost per
cold 4 KiB read. The daemon's probe plan: the boundary ruler row
(SQUEEZEFS_TEST_READ_ZERO_FILL=prefilled, 4k randread QD32x4) before and
after the boot, daemon cores per read at equal IOPS (fio is the ceiling
there), and the flat perf profile's _copy_to_iter / iov_iter_advance /
__kmalloc_noprof / kfree / _raw_spin_lock lines on the sqz-fuse-ur
class; then the full row (seam off) and THE KILL MATRIX, the zc rows
(e2e zc READ/WRITE, retention) and the dismount pass for the carrier's
orphan arm (a SIGKILLed daemon with registered slots).

Not in this patch, named for the record: the structural lever is a
READ reply that names a device range for the kernel to read into the
request's folios itself (no zero-copy slot, no READ_FIXED on the daemon
ring, no completion task work into the daemon, one daemon pass per
read); it needs the data plane's post-DMA binding recheck to become a
pre-check with an in-flight pin, a full-tier law change (charter §6.3).
